{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Swear to me.</b> After Joseph acknowledged the matter and said, ״I will do as you have spoken,״ his father did not suspect him of failing to do so. And the reason he required an oath of him was that he thought perhaps the affairs of the kingdom would preoccupy him and he would not be able to attend to his burial, or perhaps the king would not give him permission to be buried anywhere else besides Egypt, so that it would be for them an honor and a merit, and in order that the matter be firmly established in Pharaoh's eyes so that Joseph would have an opening to speak in the matter — therefore he wished to make him swear. And so it came about in the end, for he said, ״My father made me swear, saying,״ and Pharaoh answered him, ״Go up and bury your father as he made you swear.״ Thus explained the Ramban, zt\"l." "<b>And Israel bowed.</b> Since Joseph acknowledged his father's words and swore to him in this, his father bowed down to him in order to pay honor to the kingship. And on this the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״תעלא בעדניה סגיד ליה״ — he went up on his bed and bowed to him." "<b>And know that</b> the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״וישתחו ישראל על ראש המטה״ — that he bowed down to the Holy One, blessed be He, and gave Him thanks for this, for he was ill, and the Shekhinah is above the head of one who is ill, as it is said (Psalms 41): ״ה׳ יסעדנו על ערש דוי.״ And whether his bowing was to the Holy One, blessed be He, or to Joseph, in any case Scripture mentioned concerning him מטה (bed), something we do not find in connection with Abraham and Isaac, because their bed was not whole; but Jacob's bed was whole and worthy of being mentioned in the Torah. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: Jacob said, perhaps, God forbid, there is some blemish in my bed, as in Abraham, from whom issued Ishmael, and as in Isaac, from whom issued Esau. And further, Scripture mentioned מטה concerning him because the Shekhinah descended with him to Egypt, and it is written (Genesis 49): ״ויאסוף רגליו אל המטה,״ and it is written (Song 3): ״הנה מטתו שלשלמה.״ And so it says (Isaiah 58): ״כבוד ה׳ יאספך.״ And this is clear."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:07.262880127Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}